+++ b/docs/fast.md
@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+# Fast core and rendering
+
+The optional `fast` Cargo feature selects the unmodified crates.io
+`gpui-fast 0.1.13` release. The default build uses `gpui-pre 0.3.11`.
+The Fast release itself uses GPUI Pre support crates from snapshot 0.3.11.
+No core patch, Git dependency, or environment-variable fallback is required.
+
+```toml
+[dependencies]
+gpui-fast = { package = "gpui-fast", version = "=0.1.13", default-features = false }
+gpui-ohos = { version = "0.0.1-beta.0", features = ["fast"] }
+```
+
+This feature is currently available in this source branch; the published
+`gpui-ohos 0.0.1-beta.0` package does not yet contain it. Use a local checkout
+or a source override until a backend release includes the feature.
+
+Every crate exchanging GPUI values must select the same core: the application,
+OHOS backend, GPUI Kit, router, and optional diagnostics. GPUI Kit exposes
+`gpui-fast`; router 0.7.0 requires a backend-selection patch. Enabling only the
+platform feature while the application remains on GPUI Pre yields incompatible
+types. An application can keep Pre as its default and select Fast with:
+
+```rust
+#[cfg(feature = "fast")]
+extern crate gpui_fast as gpui;
+```
+
+## Scene support
+
+The OHOS WGPU renderer understands Fast's two additional scene formats:
+
+- Scroll-layer sprites select per-window tile textures rather than the image
+  atlas. Dirty generations invalidate affected tiles; cached tiles are reused
+  while scrolling. The cache has a 64 MiB budget, evicts inactive tiles, and is
+  reset with the renderer on resize or native-surface recreation.
+- Rounded overflow groups draw their children in order with analytic padding-
+  edge clip stacks. Nested groups, unequal radii, and paths use the same clip
+  coverage. Rasterized path batches keep separate scene ranges.
+
+The implementation adapts the official `gpui-fast-wgpu 0.1.13` frame graph,
+clip protocol, and tile cache to the OHOS vertex-buffer renderer. It retains
+OHOS native-surface handles, GLES adapter corrections, shared pipeline caches,
+existing sprite atlases, and registered image-alpha/sampling flags. This does
+not add a dependency on the desktop WGPU platform or modify gpui-fast.
+Source attribution and the Apache license are in `src/ohos/fast/`.
+The exact Fast version is pinned because these scene formats are private.
+
+## Validation
+
+Cross-compile both selections with the OHOS Rust toolchain:
+
+```sh
+ohrs cargo --disable-target -- check --release --target aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos
+ohrs cargo --disable-target -- clippy --release --target aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos --features fast -- -D warnings
+```
+
+The host GPU harness compiles the production renderer, shader, atlas and frame
+recording modules without OHOS N-API. It compares direct and tiled pixels for
+quads, images, glyphs and paths; verifies cache invalidation/eviction; and draws
+real GPUI rounded-overflow elements, including rounded clips inside tiles.
+A working Metal or Vulkan adapter is needed for the pixel tests.
+
+```sh
+cargo test --manifest-path tests/renderer/Cargo.toml
+```
+
+The included example selects the backend's core through `gpui-ohos` and
+supports the same optional feature. Check either selection with:
+
+```sh
+ohrs cargo --disable-target -- check --release --target aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos --manifest-path example/Cargo.toml
+ohrs cargo --disable-target -- check --release --target aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos --manifest-path example/Cargo.toml --features fast
+```
+
+Native-surface lifetime and GLES behavior must also be checked on an OHOS
+application. The example can be packaged with or without its `fast` feature.
